#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "0e45ac96-0eee-51a3-83e0-192582dcf57d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4765" :question/problem "Given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/98496fe489edc905b587f42825bd0ef76dffa68bece7e204abae6de259501835.png) find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "e1d1f80c-70d1-566d-9cc0-94899b0fa05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4767" :question/problem "Given \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/4a8b4b988e2f4c93881f577ce12f2aa59a4dbcc00bf4122e23873255c27c2d72.png), find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/id #uuid "ea39d5ec-9c8f-572d-8708-fdd97d0c04bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6184" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/f07498a45503a02fa9fb4d3ecfd9bdae54a363b632152016f2f4e5c3f6170656.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/300278e3edb64ab9dcb945668b923f385b9873be5bd33264a7ed10f6072262b7.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/e26513d3753cd1437a42a9f57f160fb25e2fb9b84e1df1db07579429749eea2c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/98d64291b8e0d4effe6bb1f3006c3d88d2687697355a83c1b092cdfb54ba4188.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/10a251d4445a3b190e92fa179badfb5e989b577f3c88812334e921522e7a20e5.png)\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/515f139b62a15ee4b38066e6225066b527e277e904ea97473d2a9f50f0f595cb.png)\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058701/assets/e377b0ff63ac6d7b3938187e47a3b5bca0087ddb6eee1d2c086835f394cdf6b9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67409960-aebe-50ac-b4a4-7097a63d6b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338169} {:answer/id #uuid "456d8242-c3b8-5c4e-b356-e2b5bfd3a0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338170} {:answer/id #uuid "864658f6-e9b1-5682-a994-b1b566adc814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338171} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cf3bcb-a448-5c8e-be86-51e491d37e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338172} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b1980c-bb75-57a0-8541-483bc1e37d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "456d8242-c3b8-5c4e-b356-e2b5bfd3a0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338170} :answer-field/id #uuid "c885e955-93bd-5f75-8eee-6a7f3152fde2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ac476d2-1a16-503d-a9ca-4392d9e5325c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106751" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab79002-3c00-51dc-8907-966060466466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338176} {:answer/id #uuid "f39afb20-b8fe-5770-9325-1520ee79e163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338177} {:answer/id #uuid "1addb499-24f9-5b37-80d1-ac2bc44aec9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338178} {:answer/id #uuid "666bf478-77e6-5fa3-a536-722fbf8df55c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338179} {:answer/id #uuid "edaaa3ae-5e08-59d7-b8b9-5f5467c92e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f39afb20-b8fe-5770-9325-1520ee79e163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338177} :answer-field/id #uuid "51eabc15-41f1-54cf-ae94-1960ccd51024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "561c8b40-300f-5972-9cb7-d1c7e137d94c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106756" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4db5ca9d-e1a1-593d-ab08-498091ef6757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338183} {:answer/id #uuid "ecebc37c-b4b0-5192-a77d-8d6fdff65b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338184} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae59ec4-55d1-538c-b999-1c598ede39fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338185} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c9641b-cc93-5f41-8ae4-8a4eaff592fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338186} {:answer/id #uuid "1a53941a-5fd0-53dc-9ad8-c692e621ac8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3c9641b-cc93-5f41-8ae4-8a4eaff592fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338186} :answer-field/id #uuid "9958143f-c7e7-53e8-afc2-f454da6b186a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "379569de-64d9-5eb2-98d5-cc05e5335c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-106762" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 15 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 15 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-5,{u}_{22}=6,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-30.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e587c53-0bc5-596a-9d98-65308425290f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338190} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8dbc26-713e-54b4-b13f-1324d7b41cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338191} {:answer/id #uuid "007cd6b9-5961-5cca-9e2f-b9a12752da6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338192} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bbd2a9-bed0-575d-8cc3-0d6aadb877ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338193} {:answer/id #uuid "efa6632f-d057-544f-839d-6882be353d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e587c53-0bc5-596a-9d98-65308425290f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338190} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d48022-7300-5aa5-90f6-d0a03764a0ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b8d761f-f823-50d6-82e7-47b259a96a25" :question/math-academy-id "q-106765" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 14 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 14 & -5\\end{aligned}] & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2b1421-af9c-5bec-a630-9164350b2be1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338197} {:answer/id #uuid "361797cd-137d-5e42-87d2-b023ea8ea21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338198} {:answer/id #uuid "15ec6b14-7dd6-5a03-aa58-afcb3c7cf5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338199} {:answer/id #uuid "d96ca6f3-b8c8-5533-83be-a723c1b960cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338200} {:answer/id #uuid "f07c2d41-adc6-56db-a067-36b9c88cec3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f07c2d41-adc6-56db-a067-36b9c88cec3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338201} :answer-field/id #uuid "78971209-7605-5746-ae21-522e9b2b96f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "880eeca1-bf28-5a19-9291-63e2792d3636" :question/math-academy-id "q-106767" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -18\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144b9931-529e-5030-8899-a1a14285ec85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338204} {:answer/id #uuid "1a9b6684-676a-5316-8b89-c21048f9817e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338205} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe74d1c-3fe0-5b70-b4a2-0dc7e6af12fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338206} {:answer/id #uuid "003d5ba5-539f-58e1-a269-17d70fbd49f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338207} {:answer/id #uuid "356da871-dd71-59da-a59a-f1dcd68bd00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "144b9931-529e-5030-8899-a1a14285ec85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338204} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5ac3a49-bef1-5ac8-b37b-0ac4b9db9264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca3291e1-ad97-58bc-a702-8c4b40e020a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-106769" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce847ad5-efcf-56ea-ab58-0cc910bb43aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338211} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a4eb97-c463-5e38-8b24-eda5c79d200a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338212} {:answer/id #uuid "a8dfee06-ca4f-5386-ba08-daac0a03cd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338213} {:answer/id #uuid "21977951-5c35-5439-a7b2-85e5ff355404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338214} {:answer/id #uuid "756236d5-5108-5aa9-a367-3e0a5eefcc57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce847ad5-efcf-56ea-ab58-0cc910bb43aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338211} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d152f78-bc62-5b74-afa1-96883f1d6ca8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3a53935-9a29-51e4-b6cc-60f8abed976b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106773" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f1aa48-56e9-50bd-913a-a86035020cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117229} {:answer/id #uuid "420525a8-83ac-5d3c-a47a-051b310d6d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -16 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117230} {:answer/id #uuid "5e36d3c8-6349-5e0d-9791-13e937e9836b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117231} {:answer/id #uuid "6a82d757-cd2a-55c4-a3c6-1b911242d528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117232} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6aff11-5c60-5ce9-8ddb-3fc56281a507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99f1aa48-56e9-50bd-913a-a86035020cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117229} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c61e6d7-1ff8-54d2-b73c-05b52de377ca" :answer-f